<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PATCH (OSM stage): Flinders St eastbound through Swanston St is open to cars.

  OSM way 724454905 (Flinders Street, ~25 m, node 3313134384 to node 2187874243, the Swanston St
  intersection) is tagged access=no, so the extract drops it and Flinders St eastbound (ways
  1093113348, 724454904) dead-ends ~25 m before Swanston St.

  Why the tag is stale: OSM history shows access=no was added in changeset 74457206 (2019-09-13)
  as a temporary Metro Tunnel construction closure; the changeset comment says Flinders St
  eastbound would be closed "for 3 yrs starting Oct 2019". The tag was never removed afterwards.

  Evidence that it is open now: Vicmap Transport (CC BY 4.0) segment pfi 25076381 runs through to
  the Swanston St node and on east: class 3 arterial, two-way, vehicular_access=1, no restriction
  (data current to 2026-10-20). Decision: PLAN.md Decisions log, 2026-10-27 ("Flinders St eastbound
  through Swanston St is open to cars", George).

  What this adds: one edge, eastbound only, from the signal node 3313134384 to node 2187874243,
  where Flinders St continues east (way 581717496). OSM tags the way oneway=no with 2 lanes, but a
  westbound direction could go nowhere: at 3313134384 the only other way is the one-way eastbound
  724454904 (Flinders St westbound has its own carriageway). So: 2 eastbound lanes, the way's
  maxspeed (40 km/h) and its highway type. The pedestrian crossing 2383211240 and the tram
  crossing 12517231691 are not in the car extract and are not modelled. The signal node
  3313134384 (traffic_signals:direction=forward) is the eastbound stop line of the Flinders /
  Swanston St intersection: stop_line_signals.nod.xml makes it a plain priority node, so the
  intersection's own signal program controls this edge and cars stop once.

  Its lanes record origId 724454904, the Flinders St eastbound way this edge extends (same name,
  lanes and limit): the overridden way 724454905 is not in the car extract, and every edge must
  name an extract way. The edge id names the overridden way so it can be traced
  (sim/checks/connectivity.py lists it under overridden_by_decision).
-->
<edges>
    <edge id="override_724454905" from="3313134384" to="2187874243" type="highway.secondary"
          numLanes="2" speed="11.11" name="Flinders Street">
        <lane index="0"><param key="origId" value="724454904"/></lane>
        <lane index="1"><param key="origId" value="724454904"/></lane>
    </edge>
</edges>
